<article class="env-definition" id="136B68">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/136B68">Definition <span data-tag="136B68">5.5.8.23</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category which admits small colimits, and let $S$ be a collection of objects of $\calC $. We will say that $S$ is a <em>set of compact projective generators for $\calC $</em> if the following conditions are satisfied: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
Each element of $S$ is a compact projective object of $\calC $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The full subcategory of $\calC $ spanned by the elements of $S$ is essentially small. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(3)$"><p>
The set $S$ generates $\calC $ under small colimits. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
  <p> We will say that $\calC $ is <em>projectively generated</em> if there exists a set $S$ of compact projective generators for $\calC $.projectively generatedgenerator!projective </p>
